NAME
    usermod - modify a user's group memberships (root only)

SYNOPSIS
    usermod <user> [-g <group|gid>] [-G <group>[,<group>...]]

DESCRIPTION
    ROOT ONLY. Either flag alone, or both together.

    -g <group|gid>   Set the PRIMARY group, in /etc/passwd. This is the gid the
                     kernel carries in the task's identity word. The group may
                     be a name (resolved via /etc/group) or a number. The
                     account's uid, home, and password are preserved.

    -G <list>        Set the SUPPLEMENTARY groups, in /etc/group. The
                     comma-separated list becomes the user's COMPLETE
                     membership: they are removed from any group not listed.
                     `-G ""` clears them. A name that doesn't exist is reported
                     and skipped; the rest still apply.

    Supplementary groups are real permissions, not bookkeeping: login and su
    hand the list to the kernel (SET_GROUPS), a spawned command inherits it, and
    the filesystem server grants the group triad if a file's group is the
    caller's primary group OR any supplementary one. Up to 8 per user.

    A change takes effect at the user's next login (or su) - it sets the group
    list on the task, so a session already running keeps the list it started
    with.

EXAMPLES
    # groupadd staff
    # usermod user -g staff              make staff the primary group
    # usermod user -G staff,devs         member of staff and devs as well
    # usermod user -G ""                 no supplementary groups
    # id                                 (as that user, after logging in again)
    uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user) groups=1000(user),1500(staff)

SEE ALSO
    useradd, groupadd, passwd, id
